.TH POWERPROFILE-X 1 "September 2026" "powerprofile VERSION" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
powerprofile\-x \- follow the power profile and set the display refresh rate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B powerprofile\-x
.RB [ \-\-once ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-state
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B powerprofile\-x
is the user half of
.BR powerprofile (1).
It runs inside your X session, follows the profile
.B powerprofile
applied last, and sets the refresh rate of the internal panel from the
.B refresh
setting of that profile, or of
.B [default]
if the profile has none.

.PP
It needs no privileges, no
.B XAUTHORITY
and no output name: it uses your own X connection and finds the internal
panel by itself, the first connected output named
.BR eDP* ,
.B LVDS*
or
.BR DSI* .
The rate is chosen among the modes the panel has at its current resolution:
.B max
picks the highest, a number picks the closest.

.PP
It waits on the state file with inotify, so it uses no CPU between profile
changes. The refresh rate is applied when the profile name changes and at
startup, never when the same profile is applied again, so a manual
.BR xrandr (1)
change stays. It exits when the X server does.

.PP
Start it from your window manager's autostart.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-once
Apply the current profile once and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read FILE instead of
.IR /etc/powerprofile.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-state " FILE"
Follow FILE instead of
.IR /run/powerprofile\-last .
.TP
.BI \-\-output " NAME"
Use this output instead of the internal panel.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Log what is being done to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR powerprofile (1),
.BR powerprofile.conf (5),
.BR xrandr (1)
